	<key>readme</key>
	<string>## Usage

See and jump to every agent running in the Herdr app, from anywhere on the Mac.

List every agent and jump to one via the `hda` keyword.

* &lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Jump to the chosen agent.
* Type to narrow the list; all words must match.

Blocked agents come first, then working, done and idle, with sub-agents listed under their parent. On
a jump Herdr comes to the front, selects the agent's space and tab and focuses its pane; an agent
with no pane, such as a parked sub-agent, opens as a peek instead. The list refreshes once a second
while it is open.

Jump to the agent that most needs attention via the `hdn` keyword.

* &lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Jump to that agent.

Agents already on screen in the current Herdr tab are skipped, so a repeated press steps on. An
optional hotkey trigger for the same jump ships unassigned; set a key on it in Alfred.

Send a follow-up prompt to an agent via the `hdr` keyword.

* &lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Switch to the agent.
* &lt;kbd&gt;⌘&lt;/kbd&gt;&lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Send the message.

The form is `hdr &lt;agent&gt; &gt; &lt;message&gt;`. Only an agent that finished its turn (`done` or `idle`)
takes a message; a blocked one has to be answered in Herdr first.

Open the floating agents panel via the `hdp` keyword.

* &lt;kbd&gt;⎋&lt;/kbd&gt; Close the panel.

The panel lists the agents and refreshes every 2 s. It stays above other apps, remembers its position
between launches, and clicking a row jumps to that agent the same way &lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; does in `hda`.

The four keywords are defaults and can be changed in the workflow's Configure Workflow sheet.

## Requirements

- Alfred 6 with the Powerpack.
- The Herdr app, opened once from Applications (it links `herdr-app` into `~/.local/bin`), and
  Herdr Core (`hc`, also in `~/.local/bin`).
- jq: built into macOS 15 and later; on macOS 12–14 install it with Homebrew.</string>
